𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “A developer runs a "kubectl" command but receives "Forbidden". How will you troubleshoot the Kubernetes RBAC permission issue?”

In production, I wouldn't immediately grant cluster-admin access. First, I'd identify which identity is making the request and what permission is missing.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Identify the current context

kubectl config current-context
kubectl auth whoami

Confirm the cluster and identity being used.

2️⃣ Check the exact permission

kubectl auth can-i get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n <namespace>

These commands help determine whether the current identity is authorized to perform the requested actions.

3️⃣ Inspect Roles and RoleBindings

kubectl get role,rolebinding -n <namespace>
kubectl describe rolebinding <binding-name> -n <namespace>

Check whether the correct user, group or ServiceAccount is bound to a Role with the required permissions.

4️⃣ Check ClusterRoleBindings

kubectl get clusterrole,clusterrolebinding

For cluster-wide resources or permissions, verify the relevant ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding.

5️⃣ Verify the permission scope

Remember:

🔹 Role — Grants permissions within a namespace.
🔹 ClusterRole — Can define namespace-level or cluster-wide permissions, depending on how it is bound.
🔹 RoleBinding — Grants permissions within a namespace.
🔹 ClusterRoleBinding — Grants permissions across the cluster.

6️⃣ Apply the least-privilege fix

Ask the cluster administrator to grant only the required permissions using an appropriate Role or ClusterRole and binding.

Retest with "kubectl auth can-i" and verify the original operation.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Forbidden usually points to an authorization issue, not necessarily a connectivity problem.

Identify the user, resource, verb and namespace before changing permissions. Avoid granting excessive access just to make the error disappear.
